  "description": "A quick trick that transforms instant ramen into rich, creamy tonkotsu-style ramen using gelatin, lard, and milk.",
  "notes": "Bloom the gelatin in room-temperature liquid first to prevent clumping. If you add gelatin directly to simmering water, it will clump up. Add the milk after bringing the broth to a rolling boil and at the same time as the noodles to avoid a foamy ramen cappuccino. If you can't find pork-flavor instant ramen, beef or chicken work too. Using lard is ideal, but you can substitute another animal fat, such as chicken or duck fat. In a pinch, a neutral oil such as sunflower oil will add fattiness but have less body and no flavor. For chashu slices, prepare in advance. Related recipe: https: //www.seriouseats.com/rich-and-creamy-tonkotsu-ramen-broth-from-scratch-recipe",
